WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.296 --> 00:00:03.840
Impossible Foods might be

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:06.470
the world's most ambitious company.

00:00:06.470 --> 00:00:09.370
Just listen to founder and CEO, Pat Brown.

00:00:09.370 --> 00:00:11.440
People wonder how big the plant-based

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:12.490
meat industry's gonna be.

00:00:12.490 --> 00:00:14.890
It will be the meat industry in 20 years.

00:00:14.890 --> 00:00:17.167
We're gonna look back, and basically say,

00:00:17.167 --> 00:00:19.350
"Wow, remember back in the day when we

00:00:19.350 --> 00:00:21.000
used to use animals to make meat?"

00:00:21.970 --> 00:00:23.930
Yes, Impossible's stated goal

00:00:23.930 --> 00:00:26.800
really is to replace the use of animals

00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:29.520
as a food production technology globally

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:31.290
by 2035.

00:00:31.290 --> 00:00:35.520
That would mean supplanting
a $1.2 trillion industry,

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:38.773
not to mention a source of
sustenance as old as time.

00:00:39.610 --> 00:00:42.040
To be fair, the company
has got a lot of people

00:00:42.040 --> 00:00:43.513
talking about its products.

00:00:44.807 --> 00:00:46.080
So there's this thing called
the Impossible Burger.

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:47.530
It's called artificial meat.

00:00:47.530 --> 00:00:51.050
The realest, fakest burger in the world.

00:00:51.050 --> 00:00:52.450
Not bad.

00:00:52.450 --> 00:00:54.240
But before Impossible Foods

00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:55.710
can take over the world,

00:00:55.710 --> 00:00:56.990
it's going to have to overcome

00:00:56.990 --> 00:01:00.350
strong competitors,
penetrate tough new markets,

00:01:00.350 --> 00:01:02.520
and generally transform its product

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:04.120
from a conversation piece

00:01:04.120 --> 00:01:07.160
into a ubiquitous staple food.

00:01:07.160 --> 00:01:10.920
With the coronavirus turning
the world of food upside down,

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:11.950
this could be the moment

00:01:11.950 --> 00:01:15.543
when Impossible's newfangled
meat goes mainstream.

00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:22.640
Impossible Foods was founded
in 2011 when Pat Brown,

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:24.870
a longtime vegan, was nearing the end

00:01:24.870 --> 00:01:27.630
of an accomplished career in biochemistry.

00:01:27.630 --> 00:01:29.040
I've been a scientist my whole life,

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:30.740
and just came to this realization

00:01:30.740 --> 00:01:32.730
that by far, the most
important scientific question

00:01:32.730 --> 00:01:35.547
in the world is, "What
makes meat delicious?"

00:01:36.930 --> 00:01:40.510
Pat Brown, some would call
a zealot for his mission.

00:01:40.510 --> 00:01:43.470
He really doesn't hold back
when it comes to talking

00:01:43.470 --> 00:01:47.240
about wanting to completely
replace animal agriculture.

00:01:47.240 --> 00:01:50.870
Producing meat from animals
is the most destructive

00:01:50.870 --> 00:01:54.220
technology on earth, and
if we're going to avert

00:01:54.220 --> 00:01:56.490
catastrophic climate
change, then we have to find

00:01:56.490 --> 00:01:57.940
a better way to produce meat.

00:01:58.890 --> 00:02:00.780
You've probably heard about the dangers

00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:02.220
of cow flatulence.

00:02:02.220 --> 00:02:05.110
Their digestive systems
produce large quantities

00:02:05.110 --> 00:02:07.263
of the powerful greenhouse gas, methane.

00:02:08.165 --> 00:02:10.780
In fact, it's estimated
that livestock farming

00:02:10.780 --> 00:02:13.410
is responsible for nearly 15 percent

00:02:13.410 --> 00:02:16.200
of all greenhouse gas emissions.

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:21.200
Impossible claims it takes 89% less GHGs,

00:02:21.290 --> 00:02:25.950
87% less water, and 96% less land

00:02:25.950 --> 00:02:27.463
to produce its burger.

00:02:28.660 --> 00:02:30.670
The key of course, would be to get people

00:02:30.670 --> 00:02:32.100
to actually eat the thing,

00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:35.210
which is where the company's
secret ingredient comes in:

00:02:35.210 --> 00:02:37.920
heme, a genetically engineered substance

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:41.270
with a bloody iron-rich
taste like red meat.

00:02:41.270 --> 00:02:45.140
So heme is a very controversial part

00:02:45.140 --> 00:02:46.290
of the Impossible Burger.

00:02:46.290 --> 00:02:49.330
It's also what makes the
Impossible Burger what it is.

00:02:49.330 --> 00:02:51.080
There are a lot of consumer groups

00:02:51.080 --> 00:02:55.530
and anti-GMO groups that
oppose the Impossible Burger

00:02:55.530 --> 00:02:57.500
because of the heme.

00:02:57.500 --> 00:03:00.400
The studies on its safety
are pretty short term.

00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:03.220
But FDA has approved
it and the company says

00:03:03.220 --> 00:03:06.160
it's completely safe, and
there isn't any evidence

00:03:06.160 --> 00:03:07.960
that it isn't.

00:03:07.960 --> 00:03:09.540
Critics have also challenged

00:03:09.540 --> 00:03:11.940
the company's claim to health advantages

00:03:11.940 --> 00:03:13.450
over traditional meat,

00:03:13.450 --> 00:03:15.610
pointing out that while
the Impossible Burger

00:03:15.610 --> 00:03:19.590
has less fat, sodium and
calories that its beefy cousin,

00:03:19.590 --> 00:03:22.440
it's also more highly processed.

00:03:22.440 --> 00:03:23.960
But let's be real,

00:03:23.960 --> 00:03:26.400
we're talking about
mimicking hamburgers here,

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:28.130
not kale salads.

00:03:28.130 --> 00:03:30.380
These burgers are definitely
better for the environment,

00:03:30.380 --> 00:03:31.260
so that's a plus,

00:03:31.260 --> 00:03:34.293
but they are not necessarily
better for your health.

00:03:35.780 --> 00:03:37.110
After years in the lab,

00:03:37.110 --> 00:03:38.610
Pat and his colleagues were confident

00:03:38.610 --> 00:03:41.430
they had a product even
Ron Swanson could love,

00:03:41.430 --> 00:03:43.550
and the Impossible Burger made its debut

00:03:43.550 --> 00:03:44.703
in 2016.

00:03:46.620 --> 00:03:49.530
Their burger began its life in the public

00:03:49.530 --> 00:03:52.753
when it was sold at
David Chang's Momofuku,

00:03:53.772 --> 00:03:54.900
and they went from sort of

00:03:54.900 --> 00:03:57.110
higher-end fine dining restaurants

00:03:57.110 --> 00:04:00.080
into higher end burger restaurants,

00:04:00.080 --> 00:04:02.820
like Bareburger and Umami Burger.

00:04:02.820 --> 00:04:06.140
And then suddenly, they
started popping up everywhere.

00:04:06.140 --> 00:04:07.480
This tastes like a burger.

00:04:07.480 --> 00:04:08.360
It tastes like meat.

00:04:08.360 --> 00:04:09.480
This is all made from plants.

00:04:09.480 --> 00:04:10.420
It's all made from plants.

00:04:10.420 --> 00:04:11.840
This tastes like beef.

00:04:11.840 --> 00:04:13.400
Doesn't that look like real meat?

00:04:13.400 --> 00:04:14.560
So meatlike.

00:04:14.560 --> 00:04:17.130
They always say it's just like a burger.

00:04:17.130 --> 00:04:18.920
It tastes like... yuckers.

00:04:18.920 --> 00:04:21.590
Beef farmers of the world, relax.

00:04:21.590 --> 00:04:24.150
No, doesn't taste like a burger.

00:04:24.150 --> 00:04:26.140
Opinions differed on the Impossible

00:04:26.140 --> 00:04:28.340
Burger's relative meatiness,

00:04:28.340 --> 00:04:32.240
but the debate itself was
fantastic free advertising,

00:04:32.240 --> 00:04:35.150
helping to propel
Impossible into major chains

00:04:35.150 --> 00:04:39.000
like White Castle, and
then, in the summer of 2019,

00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:41.260
into more than 7,000 Burger Kings

00:04:41.260 --> 00:04:42.523
across the U.S.

00:04:43.670 --> 00:04:47.230
To date, the company has
raised over $1 billion

00:04:47.230 --> 00:04:49.460
and a funding round in
March valued the company

00:04:49.460 --> 00:04:51.060
at around $4 billion.

00:04:52.680 --> 00:04:55.930
But fake meat is a
happening space these days

00:04:55.930 --> 00:04:59.240
and Impossible faces some
daunting competition.

00:04:59.240 --> 00:05:00.570
Time now for the stock of the hour,

00:05:00.570 --> 00:05:01.960
and that is Beyond Meat.

00:05:01.960 --> 00:05:04.830
Shares of the faux meat or
plant-based protein company

00:05:04.830 --> 00:05:06.570
are soaring today.

00:05:06.570 --> 00:05:10.860
Beyond Meat is also
making a very meaty burger

00:05:10.860 --> 00:05:12.640
from plants.

00:05:12.640 --> 00:05:16.280
They're very non GMO, they
stress that all the time,

00:05:16.280 --> 00:05:18.660
to differentiate
themselves from Impossible.

00:05:18.660 --> 00:05:20.210
It'd be much easier to genetically modify

00:05:20.210 --> 00:05:23.240
plant material to make it take on

00:05:23.240 --> 00:05:27.380
the texture, appearance and
aroma of the animal protein,

00:05:27.380 --> 00:05:28.570
but we won't do that.

00:05:28.570 --> 00:05:31.120
Beyond Meat had one of the biggest IPOs

00:05:31.120 --> 00:05:34.410
of the year in 2019, so Impossible,

00:05:34.410 --> 00:05:36.070
which is still privately held,

00:05:36.070 --> 00:05:38.216
has some catching up to do.

00:05:38.216 --> 00:05:39.630
But you won't hear Pat Brown

00:05:39.630 --> 00:05:41.560
knocking his biggest competitor.

00:05:41.560 --> 00:05:43.180
I wish them nothing but success.

00:05:43.180 --> 00:05:44.730
They have the same mission we have.

00:05:44.730 --> 00:05:47.620
The only rival we care
about is the incumbent

00:05:47.620 --> 00:05:49.203
animal-based industry.

00:05:50.160 --> 00:05:52.900
Rivals or not, Impossible and Beyond

00:05:52.900 --> 00:05:55.310
certainly agree on one thing.

00:05:55.310 --> 00:05:59.363
The true prize isn't the
American market, it's China.

00:06:00.660 --> 00:06:04.230
27% of the world's meat
is consumed in China

00:06:04.230 --> 00:06:07.300
compared to just 9% in the U.S.

00:06:07.300 --> 00:06:09.123
The Chinese population eats a ton of meat,

00:06:09.123 --> 00:06:11.650
they're a huge market.

00:06:11.650 --> 00:06:13.970
China's a really interesting case because

00:06:13.970 --> 00:06:16.910
they have long had vegetarian cuisine

00:06:16.910 --> 00:06:19.970
as part of their culinary culture,

00:06:19.970 --> 00:06:22.680
and whether they are looking
for meat replacements

00:06:22.680 --> 00:06:25.640
is still an open question.

00:06:25.640 --> 00:06:27.500
What we found in our reporting,

00:06:27.500 --> 00:06:30.460
is that some consumers
are excited about them.

00:06:30.460 --> 00:06:33.220
We just don't know if
it's gonna be enough.

00:06:33.220 --> 00:06:34.810
To improve its prospects,

00:06:34.810 --> 00:06:37.240
Impossible is developing a plant-based

00:06:37.240 --> 00:06:39.280
replacement for pork.

00:06:39.280 --> 00:06:42.400
Globally, pork is the
most widely consumed meat.

00:06:42.400 --> 00:06:46.810
It's almost 40% of all the
meat consumed globally.

00:06:46.810 --> 00:06:49.230
Number one in Europe, number one in China,

00:06:49.230 --> 00:06:50.970
so if we're gonna achieve our mission,

00:06:50.970 --> 00:06:53.960
pork is right up there.

00:06:53.960 --> 00:06:56.490
No word yet on when Impossible Pork

00:06:56.490 --> 00:06:57.513
will be available.

00:06:59.000 --> 00:07:01.740
This morning, growing
concerns about the nation's

00:07:01.740 --> 00:07:02.880
food supply.

00:07:02.880 --> 00:07:04.890
From butchers to national supermarkets,

00:07:04.890 --> 00:07:07.813
supplies now limited and prices rising.

00:07:09.800 --> 00:07:12.290
The impact of the coronavirus on the price

00:07:12.290 --> 00:07:15.030
and the availability of
meat has given a boost

00:07:15.030 --> 00:07:17.360
to companies like Impossible Foods.

00:07:17.360 --> 00:07:19.960
Over the four weeks ending April 19th,

00:07:19.960 --> 00:07:22.880
sales of plant-based meat increased.

00:07:23.580 --> 00:07:26.060
But Impossible has acknowledged
some negative effects

00:07:26.060 --> 00:07:27.900
from the pandemics as well.

00:07:28.440 --> 00:07:32.780
This being a product that
has primarily been served

00:07:32.840 --> 00:07:35.960
in restaurants, they took a pretty big hit

00:07:35.960 --> 00:07:37.620
with all the food service shutdowns.

00:07:37.620 --> 00:07:40.720
Although the company
doesn't want to say how much

00:07:40.720 --> 00:07:44.800
of a hit. They have,
however, announced some new

00:07:44.800 --> 00:07:46.560
retail rollouts.

00:07:46.560 --> 00:07:48.660
Recently, they announced
that they're going to be

00:07:48.660 --> 00:07:51.200
rolling out with Kroger supermarkets,

00:07:51.200 --> 00:07:54.460
and that's big, that's great for them.

00:07:54.740 --> 00:07:58.280
The question of whether
this is like a long term

00:07:58.280 --> 00:08:00.420
sea change for these companies,

00:08:01.760 --> 00:08:04.000
I dunno. we'll just have to wait and see.

00:08:04.620 --> 00:08:05.620
It's safe to say

00:08:05.620 --> 00:08:08.420
Impossible stands a slim chance

00:08:08.440 --> 00:08:11.320
of achieving its goal
of replacing the entire

00:08:11.320 --> 00:08:14.200
meat industry, but it's early days

00:08:14.200 --> 00:08:16.400
for the science of plant-based meat,

00:08:16.400 --> 00:08:18.140
and it's not impossible
that these products

00:08:18.140 --> 00:08:20.280
could one day contribute to a greener,

00:08:20.280 --> 00:08:22.340
more humane future.

00:08:22.380 --> 00:08:25.220
In the meantime, as the
technology improves,

00:08:25.280 --> 00:08:28.780
we get to look forward to
many more years of this.

00:08:28.900 --> 00:08:30.080
This is meat.

00:08:30.080 --> 00:08:31.080
No this is Impossible.

00:08:31.080 --> 00:08:32.600
Yeah, that's what I thought.

00:08:32.640 --> 00:08:33.880
This is the meat burger.

00:08:33.880 --> 00:08:34.700
Right.

00:08:34.700 --> 00:08:35.340
Yes.

00:08:35.340 --> 00:08:36.000
Yeah.

00:08:36.000 --> 00:08:36.840
I like it better.

00:08:37.060 --> 00:08:39.200
It's not even close to a
veggie burger or tofu burger

00:08:39.200 --> 00:08:40.140
or anything like that.

00:08:40.280 --> 00:08:42.340
It's very, very much a burger.

00:08:42.400 --> 00:08:43.660
This tastes like a burger.

00:08:43.660 --> 00:08:44.900
It tastes like meat.

00:08:44.920 --> 00:08:46.240
Substantial meat.

